> This article takes place during my internship in 2022 at WEDA.

# Introduction

## Context

WEDA is a medical software company that helps doctors organize their consultations. When a new doctor joins WEDA, their existing patient data — coming from whatever software they used before, with its own database schema — needs to be imported into WEDA's own database before they can start practicing.

## The problem

That import meant mapping every column of the external database to its equivalent in WEDA's schema by hand: for each new doctor onboarded, someone on the data recovery team had to go through both schemas column by column, figure out which external field corresponded to which WEDA field, and write the migration accordingly. With dozens of columns per database and a new database to map for every onboarding, this manual process was slow and repetitive, and directly delayed how quickly a new doctor could start using WEDA.

# My Solution

## Mapping the databases automatically

Without being asked, I took the initiative to prototype an automated mapping tool — "hetero db" — to match columns between an external database and WEDA's database using NLP instead of manual review. For every pair of candidate columns, I computed a trust/confidence coefficient as a weighted combination of three signals:

- attribute name similarity
- attribute synonym similarity (via NLP)
- attribute type compatibility

With this coefficient, the tool could rank, for each WEDA column, which external columns were the most likely matches. In practice, this mapped all 60 columns of an external database to WEDA's schema in about five minutes, versus the hours it used to take by hand. The resulting mapping then fed into SQL templates I wrote, which generated the full migration SQL directly from the mapping instead of it being written by hand.

## What stayed manual

The confidence coefficient isn't always decisive — some attribute pairs score below a reliable threshold and genuinely need a human decision. For those cases, I built an interactive CLI that presents the candidate matches for a given column, sorted by confidence score, so the team can pick the right one in seconds instead of searching through the schema. If a match is still unclear, they can skip it and keep going — the tool tracks what was skipped and comes back to those unresolved columns once everything else is done.

# Conclusion

This tool removed most of the manual workload from the data recovery team, letting them focus on higher-value work instead of column-by-column data migration for every new doctor. It also shortened onboarding time significantly: a new doctor coming from another company can now have their data imported almost instantly and start practicing the same day, rather than waiting on a manual import.

It's also built to stay useful over time: since the matching runs on the live schemas rather than a one-off mapping, a schema change on either side (WEDA's or the external software's) can be detected and automatically trigger a new mapping pass.
